(III. APPROVAL OF A RESOLUTION PLAN DOES NOT DISCHARGE THE SURETY) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. The Hon'ble Supreme Court has further held that the approval of a) Tj
T*
(resolution plan in respect of the corporate debtor does not discharge the) Tj
T*
(guarantor. In Lalit Kumar Jain v. Union of India & Ors., \(2021\) 9 SCC 321,) Tj
T*
(the Court held at paragraph 125 that "Approval of a resolution plan relating) Tj
T*
(to a corporate debtor does not operate so as to discharge the liabilities of) Tj
T*
(the guarantors of that corporate debtor. The guarantee is an independent) Tj
T*
(contract, and the surety's liability under Section 128 of the Indian Contract) Tj
T*
(Act is not displaced merely because the principal debt has been restructured) Tj
T*
(through the resolution process.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6. The same principle has been restated by a three-Judge Bench. In Pooja) Tj
T*
(Ramesh Singh v. State Bank of India & Anr., 2026 INSC 668, the Court held at) Tj
T*
(paragraph 18 that "The protection afforded under Section 14 of the Insolvency) Tj
T*
(and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 is corporocentric and designed solely to preserve) Tj
T*
(the asset pool of the Corporate Debtor during the corporate insolvency) Tj
T*
(resolution process. It does not operate as a personal immunity umbrella for) Tj
T*
(independent personal guarantors whose liability under Section 128 of the) Tj
T*
(Indian Contract Act remains strictly co-extensive and enforceable without) Tj
T*
(awaiting the outcome of the CIRP." That decision is directly binding on this) Tj
T*
(Adjudicating Authority.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(IV. THE CLEAN SLATE PRINCIPLE DOES NOT ASSIST THE GUARANTOR) Tj
T*
